# Component state

A drawn state (`rest`, `hover`, `active`, `open`, `focus`, `disabled`, `pressed`, or a selected variant such as `Checked=True`) is a component state. `ComponentState` in `process/modules/write-bricks/components/states.mjs` is the enum. A layer name is not a state.

CSS that copies the selected paint onto one item, and leaves it there after another item is chosen, is not a state.

For every state the visitor can enter:

1. Compare the selected item with a resting item. Paint that differs moves with the state: fill, stroke, text color, and a mark that exists only on the selected variant. Words and icons that identify the item stay.
2. HTML changes with the state. The class the component toggles (`brx-open`, `is-active`) and the control attribute (`aria-checked`, `checked`) move together. A control that looks selected is `aria-checked="true"` or `checked` only while that item is the state.
3. Data changes with the state when the component has another region (a tab pane, an accordion answer). The open item shows its region. A region Figma does not draw stays empty.
4. JS, when the component has it, is what moves the class and the attribute. Hiding a pane with CSS alone is not enough.

Tabs are the reference. `activeLook` pairs the open title with a resting title. Bricks moves `brx-open`. The check inside the open title is `aria-checked="true"`. The matching pane is the one with `brx-open`. After a click, the newly open item must match the item Figma drew as selected, including the attribute.
